{"query":"What's known about the 2015 Axial eruption?","answer":"**Brief answer**  \nThe 2015 eruption of Axial Seamount was captured in real time by the Ocean Observatories Initiative (OOI) Regional Cabled Array. It began on 24 April 2015, produced a ~7 km‑long pillow‑lava flow on the northern rift, generated thousands of earthquakes and acoustic explosions, caused a rapid 2.4 m seafloor subsidence, and was followed by a month‑long period of inflation as the magma chamber recharged. Subsequent geodetic monitoring shows the volcano has re‑inflated to about 85‑90 % of its pre‑eruption level and that magma supply has been waning since the eruption, pushing the next expected event 4–9 years into the future.\n\n---\n\n**Geodetic and deformation record**  \n- Bottom‑pressure and tilt instruments on the cabled array recorded a ~2.4 m drop of the seafloor during the eruption.  \n- After the event the summit caldera re‑inflated to roughly 85‑90 % of its pre‑eruption height.  \n- Between August 2016 and May 2019 eight short‑term deflation events (1–4 cm over 1–3 weeks) occurred quasi‑periodically (≈4–6 month intervals).  \n- During each deflation episode earthquake rates fell sharply and only recovered after reinflation resumed.  \n- The long‑term record indicates the magma supply rate peaked between 2011 and 2015 and has declined by about an order of magnitude since then.\n\n**Seismic and acoustic observations**  \n- The cabled seismometers detected ~8 000 earthquakes over a ~24 hour period at the start of the eruption.  \n- Hydrophones recorded impulsive sounds from explosive lava‑water interactions, distinguishing caldera explosions from those on the north‑rift flow.  \n- In the waning phase of the eruption broadband diffuse acoustic events (“submarine Hawaiian explosions”) were observed, coinciding with rising water temperatures in the caldera and a slowdown in deflation.  \n- The acoustic signals coupled strongly into the water column but only weakly into the solid Earth, highlighting the value of hydroacoustic monitoring.\n\n**ROV visual and environmental observations**  \n- The ROV ROPOS descended to ~1840 m depth on 26 July 2015 and photographed fresh pillow basalts less than three months old.  \n- Thick orange bacterial mats coated the new flow; white filamentous mats and localized warm fluid seeps (up to ~18 °C) were observed on the summit of the flow.  \n- Pillow flows up to >1 m across and several meters long cascaded down a 30° slope; the flow extended ~7 km along the northern rift.  \n- No large “snowblower” plumes were seen, unlike the 2011 eruption; instead diffuse flocculent material from numerous small flow sites was present.\n\n**Magma supply and eruption cycle**  \n- The eruption was preceded by a period of rapid magma influx that also produced the 2011 event, explaining the close spacing of the two eruptions.  \n- Post‑eruption uplift and the exponential increase in earthquakes per unit uplift suggest cumulative rock damage leading toward bulk failure as magma accumulates.  \n- The waning supply inferred from geodetic data implies the next eruption is likely 4–9 years away (i.e., sometime after 2023).\n\n**Forecasting and monitoring context**  \n- The 2015 eruption validated earlier forecasts that an eruption would occur in 2015 based on inflation trends recorded by the OOI cabled array.  \n- Real‑time data streams (seismic, pressure, tilt) were made publicly available during the event, enabling rapid community response.  \n- Follow‑up campaigns (e.g., a mid‑August 2015 expedition) were planned to repeat pressure measurements and update the time series.\n\n**Instrument inventory relevant to the 2015 event**  \n- Bottom‑pressure recorders (e.g., RS01SUM2‑MJ01B‑14‑BOTPTA301, RS03AXBS‑LJ03A‑14‑BOTPTA301) measuring seafloor uplift/subsidence.  \n- Tilt instruments (e.g., RS01SUM2‑MJ01B‑15‑OBSBBA102, RS03AXBS‑LJ03A‑12‑HYDLFA301) tracking deformation.  \n- Seismometers (e.g., EARTHSCOPE‑OO‑AXCC1, EARTHSCOPE‑OO‑AXEC2, EARTHSCOPE‑OO‑AXID1) recording earthquake activity.  \n- Hydrophones (e.g., EARTHSCOPE‑OO‑HYS14) capturing acoustic explosions.  \n- ROV ROPOS providing high‑resolution visual documentation of the fresh lava flow and associated biological mats.  \n\n**What the evidence does not establish**  \n- Direct measurements of lava temperature within the summit caldera during the eruption (no temperature anomalies were reported).  \n- The exact volume of erupted material; estimates are inferred from flow extent and visual observations.  \n- Detailed chemical composition of the erupted basalt beyond the general description as “basaltic hot‑spot magma.”  \n\nThe compiled observations from geodesy, seismology, hydroacoustics, and ROV imaging together provide the most comprehensive picture to date of the 2015 Axial Seamount eruption.","answer_model":"Groq GPT-OSS 120B","answer_citations":[{"id":"ARCADA-PAPER-dd0459999b83fdce6655","title":"Geodetic Monitoring at Axial Seamount Since Its 2015 Eruption Reveals a Waning Magma Supply and Tightly Linked Rates of Deformation and Seismicity","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2021gc010153"},{"id":"PAGE-fd641717e04f1214","title":"First Eyes On the Axial Eruption 2015","url":"https://interactiveoceans.washington.edu/07/2015/first-eyes-on-the-axial-eruption-2015/"},{"id":"OOI-ZOT-017","title":"Geodetic Monitoring at Axial Seamount Since Its 2015 Eruption Reveals a Waning Magma Supply and Tightly Linked Rates of Deformation and Seismicity","url":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2021gc010153"},{"id":"OOI-ZOT-041","title":"Explosive processes during the 2015 eruption of Axial Seamount, as recorded by seafloor hydrophones","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/2016gc006734"},{"id":"COSZO-REF-103","title":"A Tale of Two Eruptions: How Data from Axial Seamount Led to a Discovery on the East Pacific Rise","url":"https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2018.118"},{"id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7","title":"Eruption forecasts at Axial Seamount","url":"https://axial.ceoas.oregonstate.edu/axial_blog.html"}],"answer_links":[],"hits":[{"chunk_id":"PAGE-b0f8ac53e584a514-CHUNK-001","collection_id":"websites","collection_label":"websites","title":"Washington Post – A massive underwater volcanic eruption is captured in real time","score":0.015229079008882658,"citations":[{"source_id":"PAGE-b0f8ac53e584a514","title":"Washington Post – A massive underwater volcanic eruption is captured in real time","url":"https://oceanobservatories.org/2016/12/ooi-in-the-news-washington-post-a-massive-underwater-volcanic-eruption-is-captured-in-real-time/"}],"metadata":{"node_local_id":"PAGE-b0f8ac53e584a514-CHUNK-001","locator":null}},{"chunk_id":"LIT-CHUNK-207dfcdd5a2edbc4c8cc","collection_id":"literature","collection_label":"literature","title":"A Tale of Two Eruptions: How Data from Axial Seamount Led to a Discovery on the East Pacific Rise","score":0.013211025280898877,"citations":[{"source_id":"COSZO-REF-103","title":"A Tale of Two Eruptions: How Data from Axial Seamount Led to a Discovery on the East Pacific Rise","url":"https://doi.org/10.5670/oceanog.2018.118"}],"metadata":{"node_local_id":"LIT-CHUNK-207dfcdd5a2edbc4c8cc","locator":null}},{"chunk_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7-CHUNK-041","collection_id":"axial_monitoring","collection_label":"axial_monitoring","title":"Eruption forecasts at Axial Seamount","score":0.009016393442622951,"citations":[{"source_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7","title":"Eruption forecasts at Axial Seamount","url":"https://axial.ceoas.oregonstate.edu/axial_blog.html"}],"metadata":{"node_local_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7-CHUNK-041","locator":null}},{"chunk_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7-CHUNK-040","collection_id":"axial_monitoring","collection_label":"axial_monitoring","title":"Eruption forecasts at Axial Seamount","score":0.008870967741935484,"citations":[{"source_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7","title":"Eruption forecasts at Axial Seamount","url":"https://axial.ceoas.oregonstate.edu/axial_blog.html"}],"metadata":{"node_local_id":"PAGE-9901dc2fdc21d8c7-